                <section id="what-is-kikoff">
                    <h2 class="text-[24px] md:text-[28px]">What Is Kikoff Credit Builder?</h2>
                    <p class="mt-4 text-[15px] leading-7 text-slate-600">Kikoff is a US-based fintech (Kikoff Inc., San Francisco) offering a credit-builder revolving account. It is not a traditional credit card — you get a $750 credit line (Basic) or $1,500 (Premium) that can only be used in the Kikoff store to purchase financial literacy content, ebooks, and tools. You never get the cash, but Kikoff reports your monthly payment behavior and utilization as a revolving account to Equifax and Experian.</p>
                    <p class="mt-4 text-[15px] leading-7 text-slate-600">According to <a href="https://www.consumerfinance.gov/ask-cfpb/what-is-a-credit-builder-loan-en-2118/" class="underline font-medium">CFPB guidance</a> on credit-builder products, revolving accounts can help thin-file consumers establish payment history, which accounts for 35% of your FICO Score. Experian notes that <a href="https://www.experian.com/blogs/ask-experian/credit-education/score-basics/credit-use/" class="underline">credit mix and utilization</a> are distinct scoring factors that benefit from having both revolving and installment tradelines.</p>
                    <div class="mt-6 p-5 rounded-2xl bg-emerald-50 border border-emerald-100">
                        <div class="text-[13px] font-bold text-emerald-900">Key fact for US beginners:</div>
                        <p class="mt-1 text-[13px] leading-6 text-emerald-800">Kikoff performs no hard credit pull (no impact to score). You need a US address, 18+, SSN or ITIN for identity verification. No credit check means approval is easy if you have zero history — that's why it's popular for students and newcomers.</p>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <section id="how-it-works">
                    <h2 class="text-[24px] md:text-[28px]">How Kikoff Reports and Actually Builds Score</h2>
                    <ol class="mt-4 space-y-4 text-[15px] leading-7 text-slate-600 list-decimal pl-5">
                        <li><b>You open account:</b> Instant decision, $750 line appears in app. No SSN exposure in the browser — account creation happens in secure app.</li>
                        <li><b>You make a purchase:</b> Example: $10 ebook from Kikoff store. That creates a balance. According to Equifax, <a href="https://www.equifax.com/personal/education/credit-score/how-utilization-affects-credit-scores/" class="underline">utilization</a> is reported as balance / limit. $10 / $750 = 1.3% — ideal low utilization.</li>
                        <li><b>Kikoff reports payment history:</b> You pay $10 (or auto-pay). Reports as on-time payment to Equifax and Experian monthly. No late fees on Basic plan, but late payments can still be reported as late — CFPB emphasizes paying on time.</li>
                        <li><b>Score impact timeline:</b> Most thin-file users see first revolving account appear in 14-30 days. FICO requires 6 months of history for a score. Kikoff helps you meet that threshold without interest.</li>
                    </ol>
                    <div class="mt-6 p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white border">
                        <div class="text-[13px] font-bold">How to use it correctly (CFPB-aligned):</div>
                        <ul class="mt-2 space-y-2 text-[13px] leading-6 text-slate-600 list-disc pl-5">
                            <li>Keep statement balance under $30 (under 5% utilization)</li>
                            <li>Enable auto-pay — payment history is the #1 factor per CFPB</li>
                            <li>Do not open multiple new accounts at once — average age of accounts matters</li>
                            <li>Check your reports free at AnnualCreditReport.com — Kikoff should show as "KIKOFF" revolving</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <section id="pricing">
                    <h2 class="text-[24px] md:text-[28px]">Pricing: $0 Basic vs $5 Premium — Is Premium Worth It?</h2>
                    <div class="mt-6 grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
                        <div class="p-6 rounded-[20px] bg-white border-2 border-slate-200">
                            <div class="text-[12px] font-bold tracking-widest">KIKOFF BASIC — $0/MONTH</div>
                            <div class="mt-2 text-[28px] font-extrabold">$0</div>
                            <ul class="mt-4 space-y-2 text-[13px] leading-6 text-slate-600">
                                <li>• $750 revolving line</li>
                                <li>• Reports to Equifax + Experian</li>
                                <li>• No interest, no fees, no hard pull</li>
                                <li>• 1% utilization strategy built-in</li>
                                <li>• No credit monitoring</li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>
                        <div class="p-6 rounded-[20px] bg-slate-900 text-white border-2 border-slate-900">
                            <div class="text-[12px] font-bold tracking-widest text-emerald-300">KIKOFF PREMIUM — $5/MONTH</div>
                            <div class="mt-2 text-[28px] font-extrabold">$5 <span class="text-[14px] font-medium text-slate-400">/mo</span></div>
                            <ul class="mt-4 space-y-2 text-[13px] leading-6 text-slate-300">
                                <li>• $1,500 revolving line (+ higher potential utilization cushion)</li>
                                <li>• Adds VantageScore 3.0 monthly tracking</li>
                                <li>• Rent reporting add-on available ($5 extra)</li>
                                <li>• Still no hard pull, no interest</li>
                                <li>• Cancel anytime</li>
                            </ul>
                            <div class="mt-4 text-[11px] text-slate-400">Best if you need higher limit for lower utilization % and want credit monitoring without separate subscription.</div>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                    <p class="mt-4 text-[13px] leading-6 text-slate-500">Our take: Start with Basic. If you already have Self for installment, adding Kikoff Basic completes revolving mix at $0. Premium only helps if you want rent reporting or VantageScore tracking — not essential for FICO building.</p>
                </section>

                <section id="pros-cons">
                    <h2 class="text-[24px] md:text-[28px]">Pros and Cons — Honest Breakdown</h2>
                    <div class="mt-6 grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-6">
                        <div>
                            <h3 class="text-[15px] font-bold text-emerald-700">Pros</h3>
                            <ul class="mt-3 space-y-2.5 text-[14px] leading-6 text-slate-600">
                                <li class="flex gap-2"><span class="text-emerald-500">✔</span> Truly $0/month Basic — cheapest revolving tradeline in US market in 2026. No deposit like secured cards.</li>
                                <li class="flex gap-2"><span class="text-emerald-500">✔</span> No hard inquiry — critical for thin file, as per Experian: hard pulls can lower score 5-10 points temporarily.</li>
                                <li class="flex gap-2"><span class="text-emerald-500">✔</span> Low utilization automation: buying $10 each month keeps utilization 1-2% without effort.</li>
                                <li class="flex gap-2"><span class="text-emerald-500">✔</span> No interest, no hidden fees — CFPB complaint database shows lower fee complaints vs. Fee Harvester cards.</li>
                                <li class="flex gap-2"><span class="text-emerald-500">✔</span> Builds payment history from day 1 — reports monthly, helps meet 6-month FICO minimum.</li>
                                <li class="flex gap-2"><span class="text-emerald-500">✔</span> Works for ITIN holders — inclusive for newcomers without SSN history.</li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>
                        <div>
                            <h3 class="text-[15px] font-bold text-red-600">Cons</h3>
                            <ul class="mt-3 space-y-2.5 text-[14px] leading-6 text-slate-600">
                                <li class="flex gap-2"><span class="text-red-500">✕</span> Not usable anywhere else — store-only. Does not build real spending habit like Chime Visa.</li>
                                <li class="flex gap-2"><span class="text-red-500">✕</span> Only 2 bureaus — Equifax + Experian. TransUnion not reported, so not full coverage.</li>
                                <li class="flex gap-2"><span class="text-red-500">✕</span> Premium adds cost — $60/year adds up vs. free alternatives for monitoring (Experian free app).</li>
                                <li class="flex gap-2"><span class="text-red-500">✕</span> Low limit may not help utilization if you also open other cards — limit remains $750/$1500.</li>
                                <li class="flex gap-2"><span class="text-red-500">✕</span> Account closure can hurt utilization — revolving closure raises utilization ratio per Equifax guidelines.</li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <section id="who-needs-revolving">
                    <h2 class="text-[24px] md:text-[28px]">Who Needs a Revolving Tradeline? Understanding Credit Mix</h2>
                    <p class="mt-4 text-[15px] leading-7 text-slate-600">According to FICO and Experian, your score is built from 5 factors: payment history (35%), amounts owed / utilization (30%), length of history (15%), credit mix (10%), and new credit (10%). Most beginners only have one type — often an installment account like Self or a student loan.</p>
                    <p class="mt-3 text-[15px] leading-7 text-slate-600">Credit mix at 10% rewards you for having both:</p>
                    <div class="mt-5 grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-3 text-[13px]">
                        <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-white border"><b>Revolving:</b> Credit cards, Kikoff, Chime Credit Builder. Flexible balance, utilization matters.</div>
                        <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-white border"><b>Installment:</b> Self Credit Builder, auto loans, student loans. Fixed payments, fixed term.</div>
                    </div>
                    <p class="mt-5 text-[15px] leading-7 text-slate-600"><b>Who needs Kikoff specifically:</b></p>
                    <ul class="mt-3 space-y-2 text-[14px] leading-7 text-slate-600 list-disc pl-5">
                        <li>Thin file (0-2 accounts) — you have Self but no revolving. Kikoff Basic closes the gap at $0.</li>
                        <li>0 FICO score — need 1+ account reporting 6 months. Kikoff counts.</li>
                        <li>Denied for secured cards due to no income proof — Kikoff has no income requirement, no credit check.</li>
                        <li>Rebuilding after derogatory marks — need new positive revolving history alongside negatives aging off (Equifax: negatives stay 7 years).</li>
                        <li>ITIN or DACA recipients with US address who struggle to get traditional cards — Kikoff is more accessible.</li>
                    </ul>
                </section>

                <section id="comparison">
                    <h2 class="text-[24px] md:text-[28px]">Comparison: Kikoff vs Self vs Chime (US)</h2>
                    <p class="mt-4 text-[14px] leading-6 text-slate-500">We analyzed 2026 US plans. All three require US residency. No SSN exposure on ScoreUp — but each provider requires SSN/ITIN to report.</p>
                    <div class="mt-6 overflow-x-auto rounded-[20px] border bg-white">
                        <table class="w-full text-[13px] leading-5">
                            <thead class="bg-slate-900 text-white text-[11px] tracking-widest">
                                <tr>
                                    <th class="text-left p-4">Feature</th>
                                    <th class="text-left p-4">Kikoff Basic</th>
                                    <th class="text-left p-4">Self Credit Builder</th>
                                    <th class="text-left p-4">Chime Credit Builder</th>
                                </tr>
                            </thead>
                            <tbody class="divide-y text-slate-600">
                                <tr><td class="p-4 font-bold bg-slate-50">Cost</td><td class="p-4">$0/mo</td><td class="p-4">$25-150/mo (savings locked)</td><td class="p-4">$0/mo (requires Chime checking)</td></tr>
                                <tr><td class="p-4 font-bold bg-slate-50">Type</td><td class="p-4">Revolving</td><td class="p-4">Installment (CD-secured)</td><td class="p-4">Revolving secured Visa</td></tr>
                                <tr><td class="p-4 font-bold bg-slate-50">Bureaus</td><td class="p-4">Equifax, Experian</td><td class="p-4">All 3 (Equifax, Experian, TransUnion)</td><td class="p-4">All 3 (with direct deposit)</td></tr>
                                <tr><td class="p-4 font-bold bg-slate-50">Hard pull?</td><td class="p-4">No</td><td class="p-4">No</td><td class="p-4">No</td></tr>
                                <tr><td class="p-4 font-bold bg-slate-50">Can spend anywhere?</td><td class="p-4">No — Kikoff store only</td><td class="p-4">No — money locked until end of term</td><td class="p-4">Yes — Visa everywhere</td></tr>
                                <tr><td class="p-4 font-bold bg-slate-50">Best for</td><td class="p-4">Free revolving mix</td><td class="p-4">Savings discipline + installment mix</td><td class="p-4">Everyday spending + all 3 bureaus</td></tr>
                                <tr><td class="p-4 font-bold bg-slate-50">US Requirements</td><td class="p-4">18+, US address, SSN/ITIN</td><td class="p-4">18+, US address, SSN, bank</td><td class="p-4">Chime checking + $200+ direct deposit</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                    </div>
                    <div class="mt-6 p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50 border">
                        <h4 class="text-[13px] font-bold">ScoreUp Strategy: How We Combine Them (Not Financial Advice)</h4>
                        <p class="mt-2 text-[13px] leading-6 text-slate-600">For a complete $0-$25 starter stack in the US: Kikoff Basic ($0 revolving) + Self $25 plan ($25 installment with savings) + Chime ($0 when you have qualifying direct deposit). This gives you 2 revolving + 1 installment across all 3 bureaus. According to CFPB, diversifying on-time payments across types helps thin files faster than a single account. Keep utilization below 10% and never miss a payment. Check score growth with free calculators — no SSN needed on ScoreUp.</p>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <section id="build-plan">
                    <h2 class="text-[24px] md:text-[28px]">Step-by-Step: How to Use Kikoff If You Start Today (US)</h2>
                    <div class="mt-5 space-y-4">
                        <div class="flex gap-4"><span class="w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-slate-900 text-white flex items-center justify-center text-[13px] font-bold">1</span><div><b class="text-[14px]">Open Kikoff Basic</b><p class="mt-1 text-[13px] leading-6 text-slate-600">Apply via official site. Takes 5 mins. You get $750 limit instantly.</p></div></div>
                        <div class="flex gap-4"><span class="w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-slate-900 text-white flex items-center justify-center text-[13px] font-bold">2</span><div><b class="text-[14px]">Buy a $10 item</b><p class="mt-1 text-[13px] leading-6 text-slate-600">Pick any ebook/course. Creates 1.3% utilization — ideal per Experian guidance (1-9% is best, not 0%).</p></div></div>
                        <div class="flex gap-4"><span class="w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-slate-900 text-white flex items-center justify-center text-[13px] font-bold">3</span><div><b class="text-[14px]">Enable AutoPay for 5th of month</b><p class="mt-1 text-[13px] leading-6 text-slate-600">Ensures 100% on-time history. CFPB data: 1 late 30+ day payment can drop score 60-110 points for low scores.</p></div></div>
                        <div class="flex gap-4"><span class="w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-slate-900 text-white flex items-center justify-center text-[13px] font-bold">4</span><div><b class="text-[14px]">Check bureau reports after 30 days</b><p class="mt-1 text-[13px] leading-6 text-slate-600">Free at AnnualCreditReport.com. Look for KIKOFF revolving account, open date, balance. Dispute if missing via CFPB dispute process.</p></div></div>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <section id="faqs">
                    <h2 class="text-[24px] md:text-[28px]">FAQs</h2>
                    <div class="mt-6 divide-y border rounded-[20px] bg-white overflow-hidden">
                        <details class="p-5 group">
                            <summary class="font-bold text-[14px] flex justify-between cursor-pointer list-none"><span>Does Kikoff require SSN?</span><span class="text-slate-400 group-open:rotate-45 transition">+</span></summary>
                            <p class="mt-3 text-[13px] leading-6 text-slate-600">For reading ScoreUp, no SSN needed. To open Kikoff account, yes — you need SSN or ITIN to report to Equifax and Experian. ScoreUp never asks for SSN; we provide education only, per CFPB consumer education guidelines.</p>
                        </details>
                        <details class="p-5 group">
                            <summary class="font-bold text-[14px] flex justify-between cursor-pointer list-none"><span>How long does Kikoff take to affect credit score?</span><span class="text-slate-400 group-open:rotate-45 transition">+</span></summary>
                            <p class="mt-3 text-[13px] leading-6 text-slate-600">Reports in 14-30 days. FICO Score requires 6 months history. Thin-file users often see first score after 3-6 months of on-time payments, per Experian data. Keep other factors clean: low utilization, no new hard inquiries.</p>
                        </details>
                        <details class="p-5 group">
                            <summary class="font-bold text-[14px] flex justify-between cursor-pointer list-none"><span>Does Kikoff build TransUnion?</span><span class="text-slate-400 group-open:rotate-45 transition">+</span></summary>
                            <p class="mt-3 text-[13px] leading-6 text-slate-600">No, as of 2026 it reports only to Equifax and Experian. If you need TransUnion coverage, pair with Self or Chime which report to all 3 bureaus in US.</p>
                        </details>
                        <details class="p-5 group">
                            <summary class="font-bold text-[14px] flex justify-between cursor-pointer list-none"><span>Can I cancel Kikoff? Will it hurt my score?</span><span class="text-slate-400 group-open:rotate-45 transition">+</span></summary>
                            <p class="mt-3 text-[13px] leading-6 text-slate-600">Yes, cancel anytime. Closing any revolving account can increase your overall utilization ratio and shorten average age after 10 years (closed accounts stay on report up to 10 years per Equifax). CFPB recommends keeping oldest positive revolving open if $0 fee.</p>
                        </details>
                        <details class="p-5 group">
                            <summary class="font-bold text-[14px] flex justify-between cursor-pointer list-none"><span>Is Kikoff store purchase a scam?</span><span class="text-slate-400 group-open:rotate-45 transition">+</span></summary>
                            <p class="mt-3 text-[13px] leading-6 text-slate-600">Not a scam — it's how they fund reporting without charging interest. You buy educational content (usually $10) which is the balance you repay. The content is generic financial literacy. Value is in credit reporting, not product. Check CFPB complaint portal; review fees and terms before buying.</p>
                        </details>
                        <details class="p-5 group">
                            <summary class="font-bold text-[14px] flex justify-between cursor-pointer list-none"><span>Kikoff vs Self: which first?</span><span class="text-slate-400 group-open:rotate-45 transition">+</span></summary>
                            <p class="mt-3 text-[13px] leading-6 text-slate-600">If budget $0: Kikoff Basic first for revolving. If you can save $25/mo: Self for installment + savings. Ideal stack (educational, not advice): Kikoff Basic (free revolving) + Self ($25) after 1 month. Then add Chime when you have direct deposit eligibility.</p>
                        </details>
                    </div>
                </section>

